      en: `Stop for a moment and listen.
Voices. Bicycle bells. Bags being filled. Traders speaking to customers. On market days you may smell fried fish, bread, fruit or coffee.
It feels completely contemporary.
And yet the basic function of this place is centuries old.
The area around Leiden's Vismarkt has been associated with the weekly market for almost nine hundred years. Even today, every Wednesday and Saturday, the streets around the Nieuwe Rijn fill with stalls and shoppers. 
Now look at the water.
For medieval Leiden, this was infrastructure.
Before lorries and refrigerated vans, waterways carried food and merchandise directly into the urban centre. Fish, grain and other goods arrived by boat. Nearby, the Visbrug was an important crossing over the Rhine and helped turn this area into a natural meeting point for traders, farmers and customers.
Try to remove every modern convenience from the scene.
No supermarkets.
No refrigeration.
No sealed plastic packaging.
Fresh fish has to be sold quickly. Traders call out. Customers inspect the catch. Prices are negotiated.
The smell would have been stronger than it is today. The market was not a picturesque attraction created for visitors.
It was part of the city's food supply.
This is where everyday urban life happened.
And that is why the Vismarkt still works so well as a historical stop: the activity has never disappeared completely.
Visit on Wednesday or Saturday and fish is still being sold in the centre of Leiden.
One local example is Schaap's Vishandel. Gerrit Schaap opened the shop in 1938. Cor Haasnoot later worked there and took over the business in 1957, while preserving the Schaap name. Generations of the Haasnoot family have continued the business, and today it still trades on Leiden's Wednesday and Saturday markets as well as from its shop on Herenstraat. 
That creates an unusual opportunity.
History here does not require you to imagine everything.
You can participate.
Buy a herring. Order some kibbeling. Stand beside the water and watch the market continue around you.
Of course, today's hygiene, transport and commerce are completely different from those of medieval Leiden.
But the basic human action has survived:
people come into the centre...
they meet a fishmonger...
they choose their food...
and they carry it home.
The Vismarkt is therefore not merely a place where trade used to happen.
On the right day of the week, it still does.`,
