      en: `Look at the walls.
Thick.
Closed.
Small windows.
For much of its history, this building did not need to feel welcoming.
Its job was to keep people inside.
But that is not how the story began.
In the late twelfth or early thirteenth century, a heavy stone tower stood here in connection with the Counts of Holland. It was probably conceived as a fortified refuge or stronghold associated with comital power. In a town where timber buildings were still common, a massive stone tower would have made an unmistakable statement. 
Then its purpose changed.
The site became associated with imprisonment.
And in 1463, Philip the Good transferred the Gravensteen to the city of Leiden.
From that point onward, this became one of the physical centres of urban justice.
Prison.
Court.
House of correction.
And, immediately outside, punishment.
The square in front of the complex is still called Gerecht — literally “Justice” or “Court”. 
Imagine entering this building as a prisoner.
Forget everything you know about a modern prison system.
There is no assumption of comfort.
Long prison sentences were not even the dominant form of punishment for much of the medieval period.
A person might be fined.
Banished.
Beaten.
Or, in the gravest cases, condemned to death.
The building developed as those ideas about punishment changed. A cell block was added in the sixteenth century. In the seventeenth, the complex expanded again to include facilities connected with forced labour and new judicial rooms. 
Now look toward the gallery.
Judges could look out toward the place of punishment.
Execution was public.
Justice was meant not only to punish the convicted person, but also to be seen by everyone else.
The final execution associated with the Gravensteen took place in 1856. 
After that, the story becomes almost surreal.
A place built around imprisonment eventually stored books.
Later, Leiden University used the complex for academic purposes.
Students attended classes in rooms connected with centuries of punishment and law.
And the transformation is still continuing.
The university has now left. The building is owned by the municipality of Leiden. Heritage Leiden is temporarily using parts of it, while guided tours organised by the Pieterskerk have allowed visitors inside since 2025. 
The architecture has survived.
Its meaning has not stayed fixed.
Power.
Prison.
Punishment.
Scholarship.
Heritage.
The same walls have held all of them.`,
