      en: `Look at what is missing
The most powerful way to understand this location is not to focus only on what you can see.
Look at what is missing.
Before 1807, the open space occupied by today's Van der Werfpark was part of a densely built section of Leiden.
There were houses here.
Workshops.
Bedrooms.
Kitchens.
Families.
On 12 January 1807, at approximately a quarter past four in the afternoon, much of that world disappeared within seconds.
A cargo vessel belonging to skipper Adam van Schie was moored along the Steenschuur. It carried approximately 37,000 Dutch pounds of gunpowder — roughly 17.8 metric tonnes. A vessel carrying such a dangerous cargo was not supposed to be moored there.
Exactly what ignited the powder has never been established with certainty.
Stories about cooking aboard the ship and an accidental spark became part of the tradition surrounding the disaster, but they remain explanations rather than proven facts.
Then came the explosion.
Buildings on both sides of the canal collapsed or were severely damaged. Windows shattered throughout the city. Roofs were torn apart. Fires followed.
One historical assessment describes 68 nearby houses as completely destroyed, another 80 as reduced largely to their outer walls, with many more rendered uninhabitable.
And underneath those buildings were people.
Detailed historical sources commonly give a death toll of 151, with around 2,000 injured. Some modern visitor information rounds the number of dead to approximately 160, and a modern Leiden University publication gives 161. The exact total varies slightly by source, but the scale is not in dispute.
Turning numbers back into people
One hundred and fifty-one deaths can become abstract surprisingly quickly.
So consider one person.
Jean Luzac.
Luzac was a professor, lawyer and journalist. Through the Gazette de Leyde, Leiden had become an important source of international political news. European rulers, diplomats and intellectuals read reporting produced in this Dutch university city.
Luzac reported on revolutions, wars and international politics.
Then, on 12 January 1807, he became part of the news himself.
He died in the gunpowder disaster.
Professor Adriaan Kluit was another victim.
So were children, parents, servants, neighbours and people whose names rarely appear in modern accounts.
That is why this location should not be turned into disaster entertainment.
There is no need to create artificial images of terrified victims.
Historical drawings survive.
Contemporary accounts survive.
Maps survive.
Use those.
Show visitors the neighbourhood before the explosion.
Then remove the buildings.
The emptiness itself tells the story.
The Great Ruin
After the disaster, a remarkable void remained in the centre of Leiden.
It became known as the Grote Ruïne — the Great Ruin.
Parts of the devastated area remained unbuilt for decades.
That is unusual in a dense urban centre.
Cities normally fill empty land.
Buildings return.
Property is redeveloped.
Streets close their wounds.
Here, the wound remained visible for years.
Plans for a park appeared from the 1830s onward, and part of the disaster area eventually developed into what is now Van der Werfpark. Across the water, development took a different course. A nineteenth-century scientific laboratory was later constructed there, in the building now known as the Kamerlingh Onnes Building.
One disaster therefore produced two very different spaces.
On one side:
trees and grass.
On the other:
science and university buildings.
A king arrives
The Netherlands of 1807 was not the Netherlands of today.
It was the Kingdom of Holland, ruled by Louis Bonaparte — Lodewijk Napoleon, the younger brother of Napoleon Bonaparte.
Louis travelled to Leiden on the day of the disaster.
His presence among the ruins is documented in contemporary and later prints preserved by institutions including the Rijksmuseum.
Historical accounts describe him becoming involved in organising relief.
Workers and military assistance were sent.
Medical and financial support followed.
One account preserved by Oud Leiden describes the king visiting the disaster zone while rescue work was still underway.
Later, this response helped shape his reputation as a monarch who tried to identify with the Dutch population rather than acting merely as his brother's representative.
For the people digging through rubble that night, however, dynastic politics would have mattered far less than a simpler question:
was anyone still alive underneath?
The morning after
Disasters do not end when the explosion ends.
People needed somewhere to sleep.
The injured needed treatment.
Bodies had to be recovered.
Debris had to be cleared.
Families had to discover who was missing.
Businesses had to reopen.
Even Leiden's press was affected. A local newspaper explained that its office and printing facilities had been badly damaged, disrupting publication. Within days, ordinary reporting began again.
That detail is worth remembering.
History books often freeze catastrophes at the moment of impact.
The people living through them cannot.
Life continues.
A park built around absence
Now look at Van der Werfpark.
On an ordinary day you may see students sitting in the grass, children playing, dogs being walked and cyclists passing the edge of the park.
Nothing about that scene naturally suggests catastrophe.
But ask yourself a simple question:
why is there a park-sized open space here at all?
The answer begins in 1807.
Across the canal, a memorial stone in the quay marks the location associated with the gunpowder ship.
Stand near it and look toward the trees.
Now mentally remove them.
Fill the space with houses.
Walls.
Roofs.
Rooms.
People.
The disaster no longer survives here primarily as a ruin.
It survives as missing architecture.
And once you understand that, the peaceful park in front of you looks completely different.`,
